{
  id:'toefl-ibt-2', lang:'en', level:'toefl', badge:'TOEFL · iBT', title:'Đề thi thử TOEFL iBT — Đề 2',
  official:'Đúng cấu trúc phần làm trên máy: Reading 20 câu (35′) · Listening 28 câu (36′) · Writing 2 task (29′). Speaking 4 task nói vào micro — luyện ở màn «Luyện nói». Tổng điểm quy về thang 0–120.',
  minutes:100, maxScore:120, pass:0, passEach:0, plays:1,
  sections:[
    { id:'reading', name:'Reading', vi:'Đọc hiểu · 20 câu · 35 phút', parts:[
      { title:'Passage 1 — How Cities Cool Themselves', ins:'Read the passage and answer Questions 1–10.', vi:'Câu 1–10: Đọc bài về đảo nhiệt đô thị.',
        qs:[
          { t:'mc', q:'1. According to paragraph 1, the main cause of the urban heat island is', o:['waste heat from engines and air conditioners.','the materials cities are built from.','the density of population.','a lack of wind at night.'], c:1, e:'The larger factor is much duller: the materials cities are built from.',
            p:'HOW CITIES COOL THEMSELVES\n\n[1] On a still summer night a large city can be six to eight degrees warmer than the farmland around it. The effect has been measured since the early nineteenth century and is now called the urban heat island. Its cause is not, as many assume, the waste heat of engines and air conditioners, although that contributes. The larger factor is much duller: the materials cities are built from.\n\n[2] Asphalt and dark roofing absorb far more solar energy than vegetation, and they release it slowly through the night. A field of grass, by contrast, spends much of the day converting sunlight into evaporation rather than heat. A city has removed most of that evaporating surface and replaced it with stone that stores warmth and drains water away before it can evaporate at all.\n\n[3] The remedies follow directly from the diagnosis, and none of them is new. Painting roofs a pale colour raises reflectivity and can lower indoor temperatures by several degrees. Street trees do more, because they cool twice: once by shading the surface beneath them and again by releasing water vapour. Measurements in several cities put the difference between a shaded and an unshaded street at up to twelve degrees at pavement level.\n\n[4] What complicates the picture is that these measures are unevenly distributed. Wealthier districts tend to have older, larger trees, while the districts with the least canopy are often those where residents can least afford air conditioning. The heat island is therefore not a single citywide problem but a map of small, very unequal ones.\n\n[5] Planting is also slow. A tree that will shade a street in 2050 has to be planted now, survive drought and roadworks, and be watered for its first five summers. Cities that treat tree planting as a one-off announcement rather than a thirty-year maintenance commitment usually end up with a photograph and very little shade.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'2. Why does the author call the main factor "much duller"?', o:['Because dark surfaces look dull','Because it is less dramatic than machines producing heat','Because it was discovered recently','Because it applies only at night'], c:1, e:'Tác giả đối lập lời giải thích hấp dẫn (máy móc) với lời giải thích tẻ nhạt nhưng đúng hơn (vật liệu).' },
          { t:'mc', q:'3. According to paragraph 2, grass stays cooler than asphalt mainly because it', o:['reflects all sunlight','uses sunlight for evaporation instead of heat','is a lighter colour','loses heat faster at night'], c:1, e:'spends much of the day converting sunlight into evaporation rather than heat.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'4. The word "drains" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to', o:['stores','carries away','absorbs','freezes'], c:1, e:'drains water away = dẫn nước đi chỗ khác.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'5. According to paragraph 3, street trees cool a street in two ways:', o:['shade and wind','shade and water vapour','reflection and shade','reflection and drainage'], c:1, e:'once by shading the surface beneath them and again by releasing water vapour.' },
          { t:'tf', q:'6. Painting roofs a pale colour is described as a new idea.', o:['Đúng','Sai'], c:1, e:'and none of them is new — các biện pháp đều không mới.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'7. What complication is raised in paragraph 4?', o:['Trees need too much water','The benefits are spread unequally between districts','Pale roofs are expensive','Measurements are unreliable'], c:1, e:'these measures are unevenly distributed… very unequal ones.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'8. The author suggests that districts with the least tree cover are often those where residents', o:['prefer open space','can least afford air conditioning','own the most cars','have the newest buildings'], c:1, e:'Câu cuối đoạn 4 nêu đúng điều này.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'9. According to paragraph 5, what does a newly planted street tree require?', o:['Only planting','Watering for its first five summers and long-term care','Annual replacement','Removal of nearby buildings'], c:1, e:'survive drought and roadworks, and be watered for its first five summers.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'10. What is the author’s attitude towards cities that announce tree-planting schemes?', o:['Fully supportive','Sceptical unless long-term maintenance follows','Completely opposed','Neutral and uninterested'], c:1, e:'Câu cuối: end up with a photograph and very little shade — mỉa mai nhẹ.' }
        ] },
      { title:'Passage 2 — The Puzzle of Animal Play', ins:'Read the passage and answer Questions 11–20.', vi:'Câu 11–20: Đọc bài về hành vi chơi đùa ở động vật.',
        qs:[
          { t:'mc', q:'11. According to paragraph 1, why is play surprising from a biological point of view?', o:['It is rare among mammals','It is costly in energy, injury and risk','It occurs only in captivity','It disappears in adulthood'], c:1, e:'play is expensive: it burns energy, causes injuries and attracts predators.',
            p:'THE PUZZLE OF ANIMAL PLAY\n\n[1] Young mammals of almost every species play. They chase, wrestle, pounce on objects that cannot be eaten and repeat movements that lead nowhere. For a long time this was treated as a minor curiosity, but play is expensive: it burns energy, causes injuries and attracts predators. A behaviour that costly is unlikely to survive unless it does something useful.\n\n[2] The oldest explanation is practice. Play, on this view, rehearses adult skills — hunting, fighting, escaping — in conditions where mistakes are cheap. The theory is intuitive and partly supported: predator species play more than grazers, and much of their play resembles hunting. Yet it fits some observations badly. Kittens raised without any play still hunt competently, and many playful movements have no adult equivalent at all.\n\n[3] A second explanation shifts attention from skills to flexibility. On this account the function of play is to produce unusual situations — a slip, a fall, a sudden loss of control — and to train the animal to recover from them. Supporting evidence comes from studies showing that animals deliberately put themselves off balance during play far more often than chance would predict.\n\n[4] A third line of research treats play as social calibration. Rats that play together develop more stable relationships, and a rat that consistently wins every play fight will be avoided by its partners. Winning too often, in other words, is a losing strategy. This suggests play has rules, and that learning the rules matters more than the outcome of any single bout.\n\n[5] These explanations are not rivals so much as answers to different questions. Practice explains what is rehearsed; flexibility explains why the rehearsal is so untidy; calibration explains why it is usually done with a partner. What none of them yet explains is why animals appear to enjoy it.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'12. The "practice" explanation says that play', o:['rehearses adult skills where mistakes are cheap','has no function at all','only occurs in grazing animals','replaces adult behaviour'], c:0, e:'Play… rehearses adult skills… in conditions where mistakes are cheap.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'13. Which evidence in paragraph 2 works AGAINST the practice explanation?', o:['Predators play more than grazers','Play resembles hunting','Kittens raised without play still hunt well','Play is common in young animals'], c:2, e:'Kittens raised without any play still hunt competently.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'14. The second explanation focuses on training animals to', o:['win fights','recover from losing control','find food faster','recognise their own species'], c:1, e:'train the animal to recover from them (slips, falls, loss of control).' },
          { t:'mc', q:'15. What evidence supports the flexibility explanation?', o:['Animals avoid falling during play','Animals put themselves off balance more often than chance predicts','Play increases with age','Play only happens alone'], c:1, e:'studies showing that animals deliberately put themselves off balance.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'16. According to paragraph 4, a rat that wins every play fight will', o:['become the leader','be avoided by its partners','play more often','stop playing entirely'], c:1, e:'a rat that consistently wins every play fight will be avoided by its partners.' },
          { t:'tf', q:'17. The passage says the three explanations contradict one another.', o:['Đúng','Sai'], c:1, e:'These explanations are not rivals so much as answers to different questions.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'18. The word "bout" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to', o:['injury','single episode of fighting','partner','rule'], c:1, e:'bout = một hiệp, một lượt.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'19. Which question does the passage say remains unanswered?', o:['What is rehearsed in play','Why play is untidy','Why play is done with a partner','Why animals appear to enjoy play'], c:3, e:'Câu cuối: What none of them yet explains is why animals appear to enjoy it.' },
          { t:'mc', q:'20. What is the overall organisation of the passage?', o:['A problem followed by three partial explanations','A chronological history','A comparison of two species','An argument against a single theory'], c:0, e:'Đoạn 1 nêu nghịch lý, đoạn 2–4 đưa ba cách giải thích, đoạn 5 tổng hợp.' }
        ] } ] },

    { id:'listening', name:'Listening', vi:'Nghe hiểu · 28 câu · 36 phút', parts:[
      { title:'Conversation 1 — Student and Housing Office (Questions 21–25)', ins:'Listen to each item and choose the best answer.', vi:'Câu 21–25: Hội thoại giữa sinh viên và phòng ở ký túc.',
        qs:[
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'21. Why does the student visit the housing office?', o:['To pay rent','To ask about moving rooms','To return a key','To book a guest room'], c:1, e:'«I was hoping to move to a quieter room» → xin đổi phòng.', a:'Good afternoon. How can I help? — Hi. I am in Block C, room 214, and I was hoping to move to a quieter room if anything is free.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'22. What is the student’s main complaint?', o:['The room is small','Noise from the shared kitchen','The heating does not work','The rent has increased'], c:1, e:'«the kitchen is right next door and people cook until two in the morning».', a:'What is the problem with 214? — The kitchen is right next door and people cook until two in the morning. I have early lectures.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'23. What does the officer say about rooms on the top floor?', o:['They are all taken','Two are free but cost more','They are being repainted','They are reserved for staff'], c:1, e:'«Two on the fourth floor are free, but they are twelve pounds a week more».', a:'Let me look. Two on the fourth floor are free, but they are twelve pounds a week more. There is nothing at the same price until December.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'24. What does the student decide to do?', o:['Take a room immediately','Wait until December','Look at the room first','Complain about the neighbours'], c:2, e:'«Could I see one before I decide?»', a:'Could I see one before I decide? Twelve pounds is a lot on my budget. — Of course. I can show you at four today.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'25. What does the officer suggest as an alternative?', o:['Reporting the noise so it is recorded','Changing course','Buying earplugs','Moving off campus'], c:0, e:'«report it each time… it builds a record» — ghi nhận để có căn cứ xử lý.', a:'And whatever you decide, report the noise each time it happens. It builds a record, and that is what we act on.' }
        ] },
      { title:'Lecture 1 — Geology (Questions 26–31)', ins:'Listen to each item and choose the best answer.', vi:'Câu 26–31: Bài giảng địa chất.',
        qs:[
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'26. What is the lecture mainly about?', o:['How mountains form','How rivers change their course','How deserts expand','How glaciers melt'], c:1, e:'«today we look at why rivers do not stay where we put them».', a:'Right, today we look at why rivers do not stay where we put them — how and why a river changes course.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'27. According to the professor, a meander grows because', o:['water flows faster on the outer bank','the riverbed rises','rainfall increases','the banks are made of rock'], c:0, e:'«the water on the outside of a bend moves faster and cuts away the bank».', a:'The water on the outside of a bend moves faster and cuts away the bank, while the inside is slow and drops sediment. So the curve deepens.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'28. What is an oxbow lake?', o:['A lake formed by a dam','A cut-off meander left behind','A lake at a river mouth','An underground lake'], c:1, e:'«the neck is cut through and the old loop is left as a lake».', a:'Eventually the neck is cut through and the old loop is left behind as a lake. That is your oxbow.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'29. Why does the professor mention flood defences?', o:['To show they always work','To explain that straightening a river moves the problem downstream','To recommend building more of them','To describe their cost'], c:1, e:'«you have not removed the water, you have moved it downstream faster».', a:'Now, if you straighten a river to protect a town, you have not removed the water. You have moved it downstream faster, and someone else gets it.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'30. What does the professor say about older maps?', o:['They are useless','They show where a river used to run, which predicts flooding','They exaggerate river width','They were drawn from the air'], c:1, e:'«an old map tells you where the river considers home».', a:'And look at old maps. An old map tells you where the river considers home — and rivers do go home.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'31. What will the next class cover?', o:['Coastal erosion','Sediment in deltas','Groundwater','Volcanic soils'], c:1, e:'«next week: what happens to all that sediment when the river reaches the sea».', a:'Next week: what happens to all that sediment when the river finally reaches the sea.' }
        ] },
      { title:'Lecture 2 — Economics (Questions 32–37)', ins:'Listen to each item and choose the best answer.', vi:'Câu 32–37: Bài giảng kinh tế học.',
        qs:[
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'32. What concept does the lecture introduce?', o:['Inflation','Opportunity cost','Interest rates','Trade deficits'], c:1, e:'«the cost of anything is what you gave up to get it» — chi phí cơ hội.', a:'Today, one idea that sounds obvious and is almost never applied: the cost of anything is what you gave up to get it.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'33. What example does the professor use first?', o:['Buying a car','Spending a Saturday on badly paid work','Renting a flat','Choosing a university'], c:1, e:'Ví dụ mở đầu là một ngày thứ Bảy làm việc trả công thấp.', a:'Say you spend Saturday on a piece of freelance work that pays very little. The cost is not the effort. It is the course you did not take.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'34. Why does the professor say the alternative is "invisible"?', o:['Because it never happened, so its problems never appeared','Because nobody records it','Because it is illegal','Because it happens later'], c:0, e:'«had you chosen it, you would have seen its problems too».', a:'The trouble is the alternative is invisible. Had you chosen it, you would have seen its problems too — and they might have been worse.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'35. What warning does the professor give about regret?', o:['It is always useful','It compares a real life with an untested imagined one','It fades quickly','It only affects older people'], c:1, e:'«comparing a real life… against an imagined one that was never tested».', a:'So regret is a bad guide. You are comparing a real life, with all its friction, against an imagined one that was never tested.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'36. What does the professor recommend judging instead of outcomes?', o:['The amount of money involved','The quality of the decision at the time','The opinion of others','The length of time taken'], c:1, e:'«judge the decision, not the outcome».', a:'Judge the decision, not the outcome. Given what you knew and the time you had, was the reasoning sound?' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'37. According to the professor, what happens if you learn only from results?', o:['You improve faster','You will draw the wrong lesson about half the time','You become too cautious','Nothing changes'], c:1, e:'«you will draw the wrong lesson roughly half the time».', a:'Some good decisions end badly and some careless ones end well. Learn only from results and you will draw the wrong lesson roughly half the time.' }
        ] },
      { title:'Conversation 2 & Lecture 3 (Questions 38–48)', ins:'Listen to each item and choose the best answer.', vi:'Câu 38–48: Hội thoại phòng thí nghiệm và bài giảng sinh học.',
        qs:[
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'38. Why does the student email the professor?', o:['To ask for an extension','To report broken equipment','To join a research group','To change supervisor'], c:2, e:'«I read your paper and wondered whether you take undergraduates».', a:'Professor, I read your paper on soil bacteria and wondered whether you ever take undergraduates in the lab.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'39. What does the professor ask about first?', o:['The student’s grades','Whether the student can commit regular hours','The student’s year of study','The student’s funding'], c:1, e:'«can you give me the same six hours every week?»', a:'The first question is not grades. Can you give me the same six hours every week for a whole term?' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'40. Why does the professor emphasise regularity?', o:['Because experiments run on fixed schedules','Because the lab is small','Because of safety rules','Because of funding rules'], c:0, e:'«cultures do not wait for your timetable».', a:'Cultures do not wait for your timetable. If nobody feeds them on Thursday, Thursday is lost.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'41. What task does the professor offer the student at first?', o:['Designing an experiment','Washing and preparing equipment','Writing a paper','Teaching first-years'], c:1, e:'«you would start with preparation and cleaning».', a:'You would start with preparation and cleaning. It sounds dull. It is also where you learn what contamination looks like.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'42. How does the student react?', o:['Disappointed','Willing, and asks when to start','Confused','Uninterested'], c:1, e:'«That is fine. When can I start?»', a:'That is fine. Honestly, I just want to be in the room. When can I start?' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'43. What is the lecture mainly about?', o:['Bird migration','How gut bacteria affect digestion','Plant growth','Insect vision'], c:1, e:'«what your gut bacteria actually do for you».', a:'So: what your gut bacteria actually do for you, and why the answer keeps changing.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'44. According to the lecturer, gut bacteria help break down', o:['proteins only','fibre that human enzymes cannot digest','fats only','vitamins'], c:1, e:'«fibre that our own enzymes cannot touch».', a:'They break down fibre that our own enzymes cannot touch, and they release compounds we then absorb.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'45. Why does the lecturer mention antibiotics?', o:['They cure all infections','They reduce bacterial diversity for months','They improve digestion','They are no longer used'], c:1, e:'«diversity can stay reduced for months afterwards».', a:'A course of antibiotics does its job, but diversity can stay reduced for months afterwards.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'46. What does the lecturer say about probiotic products?', o:['They always work','Effects depend on the strain and are often overstated','They are dangerous','They replace a healthy diet'], c:1, e:'«the effect depends on the strain, and the marketing rarely says which».', a:'As for probiotics — the effect depends on the strain, and the marketing rarely says which one it is.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'47. What does the lecturer recommend as the best-supported advice?', o:['Taking supplements daily','Eating a wide range of plant foods','Avoiding all bacteria','Fasting regularly'], c:1, e:'«the dullest advice is the best supported: eat a wide range of plants».', a:'The dullest advice turns out to be the best supported: eat a wide range of plants.' },
          { k:'listen', t:'mc', q:'48. What is the lecturer’s attitude towards current knowledge in this field?', o:['Confident that it is settled','Cautious, noting the answers keep changing','Dismissive of the whole field','Only interested in medicine'], c:1, e:'«why the answer keeps changing» và giọng thận trọng suốt bài.', a:'Be careful with anything that sounds certain here. Five years is a long time in this field.' }
        ] } ] },

    { id:'writing', name:'Writing', vi:'Viết · 2 task · 29 phút', parts:[
      { title:'Integrated Writing Task — 20 minutes', ins:'Read the passage, listen to the lecture, then write 150–225 words summarising the points made in the lecture and explaining how they cast doubt on the reading.', vi:'Task 1 (20 phút, 150–225 từ): tóm tắt ý bài giảng và giải thích chúng phản bác bài đọc thế nào.',
        qs:[
          { k:'listen', t:'essay', min:150, words:['lecture','reading'],
            q:'READING PASSAGE (3 minutes)\n\nMany cities are replacing on-street parking with cycle lanes. Supporters give three arguments. First, cycle lanes move more people in the same space: a lane of parked cars serves a handful of drivers, while a cycle lane can carry several thousand riders a day. Second, local shops benefit, because cyclists stop more often and spend more per month than drivers passing through. Third, the change is cheap: paint and bollards cost a fraction of any new road.',
            a:'Now listen to part of a lecture on this topic. — The reading gives three reasons, and each needs qualifying. On capacity: a cycle lane can carry thousands of riders, but only where enough people are willing and able to cycle. In a hilly city, or one where the average trip is fifteen kilometres, that lane may carry a few hundred, and the capacity argument collapses. Second, the claim about shops. The studies behind it were mostly done in dense inner districts where customers already live nearby. Repeat the same study in an outer suburb where people arrive by car with a boot to fill, and the result reverses. Third, cost. Paint and bollards are indeed cheap, and that is exactly the problem. Cheap lanes are narrow, badly protected and often blocked, so they attract only the confident riders who were cycling anyway. The lanes that actually change behaviour are physically separated, need drainage and signalling, and cost far more than the reading suggests.',
            e:'Bài giảng không bác bỏ hoàn toàn mà thêm điều kiện cho từng luận điểm: sức chở phụ thuộc địa hình và cự ly; nghiên cứu về cửa hàng chỉ đúng ở khu nội đô đông đúc; và làn rẻ tiền thì không đổi được hành vi.' }
        ] },
      { title:'Writing for an Academic Discussion — 10 minutes', ins:'Read the discussion and write a response of at least 100 words that contributes your own opinion.', vi:'Task 2 (10 phút, tối thiểu 100 từ): tham gia thảo luận học thuật, nêu quan điểm riêng có lập luận.',
        qs:[
          { t:'essay', min:100, words:['I','because'],
            q:'PROFESSOR: This week we are discussing assessment. Some universities have replaced final examinations with continuous coursework; others have kept exams and argue that coursework rewards the students with the most free time. In your view, which system assesses learning more fairly, and why?\n\nMAI: Coursework is fairer. An exam measures how you performed on one morning, and a bad morning can undo a year of work.\n\nTOM: I disagree. Coursework quietly favours students who are not working twenty hours a week to pay rent. An exam at least gives everyone the same three hours.',
            e:'Bài đạt điểm cao thường: nêu rõ lập trường, phản hồi TRỰC TIẾP tên một bạn trong thảo luận, thêm một lý do hoặc ví dụ MỚI chưa ai nói, và giữ trong khoảng 100–140 từ.' }
        ] } ] }
  ]
}
